VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED

The controller-side logic is coherent and reads well: `assignHousehold` → `isHouseholdInUse(householdId, …)` → the `householdInUse && !sharesHousehold` 409 → the `declaredHouseholdMember` guard on soft-duplicate detection all sit in sensible units, and removing the now-redundant `sameHousehold` helper plus updating the javadoc/OpenAPI docs is the right cleanup. One structural issue, though:

**`assignHousehold` still carries DB-oriented scaffolding it no longer earns** (`ClinicServiceImpl.java:266-270`). Before this change the method fanned out over `ownerRepository.findAll()` and called `ownerRepository.save(existing)` to re-link owners, which is why it was `@Transactional`. After the change the body is a single pure computation:

```java
@Override
@Transactional
public void assignHousehold(Owner owner) throws DataAccessException {
    owner.setHouseholdId(householdIdFor(owner.getLastName(), owner.getPostcode()));
}
```

It touches no repository, opens no persistence work, and operates on a still-transient `Owner`. The `@Transactional` annotation is now dead scaffolding left over from the old implementation and misrepresents the method's contract — a maintainer reading it would reasonably expect DB interaction that isn't there. Drop the `@Transactional` (the uniform `throws DataAccessException` across the `ClinicService` interface can stay for interface consistency).

Worth a second thought (not blocking): since `assignHousehold` is now just a thin mutating wrapper around the private static `householdIdFor`, consider whether "assign" is still the right verb for what is now a deterministic derive-and-set, or whether the two-step "assign then read back `owner.getHouseholdId()`" in the controller would be clearer as the service returning the computed id. Either is defensible; the `@Transactional` removal is the concrete fix.

